course: geography
lesson: 18
title: Deserts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A desert is a place where very little rain or snow falls, usually less than about 250 mm a year, so a desert does not have to be hot or sandy: icy Antarctica is the biggest desert on Earth.
explain: Films show the Sahara as endless golden dunes, but only about a quarter of it is sand; most of it is bare rock and gravel plains.
explain: Many great deserts lie in belts about 30 degrees north and south of the equator, where air that has already dropped its rain over the tropics sinks and warms, so clouds cannot form.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 the Sahara looks like an empty wall between North and West Africa. Really camel caravans crossed it for centuries, carrying salt, gold, cloth and also enslaved people, linking Timbuktu with the salt mines and the Mediterranean.
explain: Think of a beach in July: the sand burns your feet even when the air feels fine, because the ground soaks up sunlight and gets much hotter than the air above it.
fact: A desert is a place that gets very little rain or snow, usually less than about 250 mm a year, so deserts can be cold as well as hot.
fact: Antarctica is the largest desert in the world, and the Sahara, at about 9 million square kilometres, is the largest hot desert, about as large as China.
fact: Only about a quarter of the Sahara is covered by sand dunes, while most of it is bare rock plateaus and gravel plains.
fact: Between about 11,000 and 5,000 years ago the Sahara was much wetter, with lakes, grasslands, hippos and crocodiles, and rock paintings there show swimmers and cattle.
fact: The Atacama Desert in Chile is the driest non-polar desert, and some of its weather stations have never recorded measurable rain; its soil is so dry and lifeless, much like Mars, that scientists test Mars rovers there.
fact: Death Valley in California holds the record for the highest air temperature, 56.7 degrees Celsius in 1913, though some experts doubt that reading.
fact: Satellites have measured ground surfaces near 70 degrees Celsius in Iran's Lut Desert, much hotter than the air above.
fact: Many great deserts lie near 30 degrees north or south of the equator, where dry air sinks after losing its rain over the tropics.
fact: Camels, which can go a long time without water, carry heavy loads and cope with heat and sand, made regular caravans across the Sahara possible, carrying salt, gold, cloth and also enslaved people between West Africa and North Africa.
fact: The Gobi Desert in Mongolia and China is a cold desert, with winters below minus 30 degrees Celsius, and some of the first known dinosaur egg nests were found there in the 1920s.
fact: Australia is the driest inhabited continent, and about 70 percent of it is arid or semi-arid land, so most Australians live near the coast.
fact: An oasis is a place in a desert where underground water reaches the surface or can be dug out easily, so palms and crops can grow.
exercise: What makes a place a desert? | It gets very little rain or snow, usually under about 250 mm a year | It is always hot, day and night, all through the year | It is covered in sand | It has no animals or plants living in it at all, not even insects or cactuses
exercise: Which is the largest desert in the world? | Antarctica | The Sahara | The Gobi in Mongolia | The Atacama Desert
exercise: Which is the largest hot desert in the world? | The Sahara | The Gobi, in Asia | The Kalahari | Death Valley
exercise: About how big is the Sahara? | About 9 million square kilometres, about as large as China | About the size of France and Spain put together | About the size of London | Bigger than all of Asia and Europe put together, by a long way
exercise: How much of the Sahara is covered by sand dunes? | About a quarter | All of it, everywhere | About nine-tenths | None of it at all
exercise: What was the Sahara like between about 11,000 and 5,000 years ago? | Much wetter, with lakes, grasslands, hippos and crocodiles | Covered by a thick sheet of ice, just like the middle of Greenland is today | Under the Mediterranean Sea | Exactly as dry as today
exercise: Which is the driest non-polar desert? | The Atacama in Chile | The Sahara in Africa | The Gobi in Asia | The Mojave in the USA
exercise: Where was the highest air temperature recorded? | In Death Valley, California, though some experts doubt the reading | In the middle of the Sahara, without any doubt at all | On the Equator in Brazil | At the bottom of a volcano in Iceland, right next to the flowing lava
exercise: Where have satellites measured ground near 70 degrees Celsius? | In Iran's Lut Desert | In Antarctica in summer | In the Gobi in winter | On the top of Mount Everest
exercise: Why do many great deserts lie near 30 degrees north and south of the equator? | Dry air sinks there after losing its rain over the tropics | They are the places on Earth that stay closest to the Sun all year round | The sea is too salty there to make rain clouds | Mountains surround all of them
exercise: Why were camels so important for crossing the Sahara? | They could go a long time without water and carry heavy loads | They could run faster than horses for days | They could swim across lakes | They knew the way to every oasis by smell and never got lost in a storm
exercise: What did Sahara caravans carry? | Salt, gold, cloth and also enslaved people | Only water and ice for the towns | Coal, iron and steel for the factories of the coast | Oil and cars
exercise: What kind of desert is the Gobi? | A cold desert, with winters below minus 30 degrees | A hot, wet desert full of palm trees and rivers | A desert under the sea | A desert made of salt only, like a big dry lake bed
exercise: What famous find was made in the Gobi in the 1920s? | Some of the first known dinosaur egg nests | The first gold coins ever made by people | A lost city of the Aztecs | The bones of the very first camel that ever lived
exercise: About how much of Australia is arid or semi-arid? | About 70 percent | About 7 percent | All of it | About 20 percent
exercise: What is an oasis? | A place in a desert where underground water reaches the surface | A mirage that looks like water | A hotel in the desert | A sand dune with a lake on top that never dries out, even in summer
test: Josh says: «Antarctica can't be a desert, it's all ice!» What is the best answer? | It is a desert because very little snow falls there each year | Josh is right, deserts must be sandy | It is a desert because it is hot | It is only a desert in summer
test: «the sahara's all sand dunes like in the films right» What is right? | Only about a quarter is dunes; most is rock and gravel | Yes, every bit of it is sand, as deep as a house | No, it is mostly grass | No, it is mostly ice and snow, up in the high mountains
test: In Europa Universalis 5 the Sahara looks like an empty barrier. What is wrong with that picture? | Camel caravans crossed it for centuries, linking West and North Africa | Nothing, nobody ever crossed it | It was a sea in 1337 | It was full of big cities, with rivers and green fields between them all
test: «why'd camels be better than horses in the desert, horses are faster» Which is the best answer? | Camels go much longer without water and cope with heat and sand | Camels are faster than horses | Horses cannot walk on sand at all, they sink straight in up to their knees | Camels eat sand
test: What would happen to a desert town if its oasis well dried up for good? | People would have to bring water from far away or leave | Nothing, people can live there quite well without water | Rain would start falling instead to fill the well up again | The sand would turn into soil
test: «how do they know the sahara was green, were they there lol» Which is the best answer? | Rock paintings of swimmers and cattle, and old lake beds, show it | A film from that time shows it | Nobody knows, it is only a guess made from looking at the sand today | The camels told them
test: «why do scientists test mars rovers in the atacama» Which is the best answer? | Its soil is so dry and lifeless that it is a lot like Mars | It is the closest place to Mars | It is the hottest place on Earth, just as hot as Mars is | It has red sand that fell down from Mars a long time ago
test: «death valley's the hottest place ever, 100 percent proven» What is the fairest answer? | It holds the record of 56.7 degrees, but some experts doubt that reading | Yes, it is proven beyond any doubt by every single expert who has looked | No, Antarctica is hotter | No, London is hotter
test: «why does the sand burn my feet when the air's not even that hot» Which is the best answer? | The ground soaks up sunlight and gets much hotter than the air above | The sand makes its own fire | The air is actually hotter than the sand | Feet are colder than the air
test: «why do most aussies live on the coast?? the outback's massive» Which is the best answer? | Most of the inland is very dry, while the coasts get more rain | The inland is covered in ice | It is against the law to live inland | The outback is under the sea
test: «ugh the gobi's a desert so it's boiling all year yeah» What is right? | No, it is a cold desert with winters below minus 30 degrees | Yes, it is hot every day | No, it is a rainforest | Yes, it never goes below 30 degrees, not even on the coldest winter nights
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the defining property of a desert? | A desert is a region receiving very little precipitation, customarily under some two hundred and fifty millimetres a year | A desert is a region of high temperature irrespective of precipitation | A desert is a region necessarily covered by sand dunes | A desert is a region devoid of all living organisms
exam: Identify the largest desert upon the Earth. | Antarctica | The Sahara Desert | The Gobi Desert | The Arabian Desert
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the surface of the Sahara. | About one quarter of the Sahara is covered by sand dunes, the remainder being chiefly rock and gravel | The Sahara is covered in its entirety by sand dunes | The Sahara consists principally of grassland | The Sahara consists principally of permanent lakes
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for the location of numerous great deserts at approximately thirty degrees of latitude north and south of the equator. | Dry air, having discharged its moisture over the tropics, descends in those latitudes, thereby inhibiting the formation of cloud | Those latitudes lie closest to the Sun | Those latitudes are encircled by mountain ranges | Those latitudes receive no sunlight
